# Files consumed by Linux tooling that rejects CRLF outright (dpkg-deb and
# rpmbuild both ship these verbatim into the package, and a CRLF shebang
# line makes a shell script unrunnable) -- force LF regardless of a Windows
# checkout's core.autocrlf setting, which would otherwise silently
# reintroduce CRLF the next time the file is touched. This rule predates
# the native .deb/.rpm pipeline (it was originally written for the
# now-removed AppImage's strict desktop-entry validator) but is still
# required by the tooling that replaced it.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.desktop text eol=lf

# Evaluated nuclear data, and the 2018 originals they were audited
# against. Pin the line endings so a byte comparison between a checkout,
# a migration archive and assets/ answers the question actually being
# asked. Without this, core.autocrlf rewrites them on checkout while a
# file copied straight out of an archive keeps LF, and `cmp` then reports
# two identical datasets as different -- which it did, costing a session.
# The parser splits on whitespace, so neither ending changes any result.
*.sou text eol=lf
